  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic overview of the catalytic scope of a single particle or catalyst, shown as a large yellow sphere in the center, with arrows linking it to many different organic transformations. Rather than giving data, it summarizes how one catalyst can convert a wide range of feedstocks into useful products.\n\nOn the left, syngas conversion is highlighted through Fischer–Tropsch chemistry, where CO and H₂ lead to mixtures such as olefins, paraffins, and oxygenates. At the bottom, the same catalyst is associated with CO₂ hydrogenation, producing products that appear to include methane and formic acid. Other arrows indicate hydrogenation or related upgrading of arenes, converting aromatic rings into saturated cyclic products.\n\nAcross the top and right, the catalyst is connected to selective functional-group transformations: nitro compounds to amines, alkynes to alkenes, and reactions involving biomass-derived sugars or polyols. The sugar drawings suggest hydrogenation or deoxygenation of highly oxygenated molecules. Overall, the image conveys a versatile catalytic platform able to process fossil- and biomass-derived molecules, as well as small carbon sources like CO and CO₂, into fuels and value-added chemicals.",
